{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak, etc., saying.</b> The reason for His saying ״לֵאמֹר״ a second time — perhaps it is because since this commandment's primary warning falls upon women, therefore He said ״לֵאמֹר״ to the women, that they should be warned in the matter. It will further be explained according to what they said in Sifra (Torat Kohanim here): ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ in this regard, and the nations are not in this regard — end quote. Therefore He said ״לֵאמֹר״ in the language of utterance and exaltation, for this matter that He conditioned to be in Israel and not in the gentile is on account of the distinction and the honor that HaShem exalted His people — which is not so with the gentile, for their soul and body are impure." "<b>A woman when she produces seed, etc.</b> One must know why it was necessary to say <b>כִּי תַזְרִיעַ וְיָלְדָה</b> and it would not have sufficed to say ״אִשָּׁה כִּי תֵלֵד זָכָר״. And our Sages of blessed memory disagreed (Torat Kohanim here): the Sages say, to exclude a birth through the wall (i.e., a Caesarean section), for the birth must take place in the place of the seed; and Rabbi Shimon says, to include a birth by Caesarean. And there is yet more for the Master of words to note: why did Scripture change its language, beginning to speak in the future tense — <b>תַּזְרִיעַ</b> — and finishing by saying <b>וְיָלְדָה</b> and not וְתֵלֵד, as it also says nearby, וְאִם נְקֵבָה תֵלֵד? And although many verses speak in this pattern — past tense in place of future tense — nevertheless a change in language tells us something. Further, why did He say <b>וְיָלְדָה</b>, which conveys certainty, and not say ״אִם תֵּלֵד זָכָר״ — for after all, there is no greater certainty for a male than for a female? And to this one would say that the ״כִּי״ of ״כִּי תַזְרִיעַ״ functions as ״אִם״ (if), and it carries forward to ״וְיָלְדָה״, but this is forced." "And our Sages of blessed memory said concerning a woman who discharges seed (Niddah 31a) and concerning one who sees [seed] (Berakhot 60a): a woman who emits seed first gives birth to a male, and therefore it says ״וְיָלְדָה״ with certainty. And although Rabbi Tzadok derived this teaching from the verse (Genesis 46:15) ״וְאֵת דִּינָה בִתּוֹ״ — Scripture suspended the males upon the females and the females upon the males — it appears that from there the reason for the suspension is not known, whereas here Scripture revealed the reason for the suspension: ״כִּי תַזְרִיעַ״ first. And there was no need to say ״תְּחִלָּה״ (first), for in any event she must produce seed, whether for giving birth to a male or to a female; and this verse did not suffice, and the verse ״וְאֵת דִּינָה בִתּוֹ״ was needed, to teach that when the man emits first she gives birth to a female. And there are yet more words for the Master." "It will further be explained by way of the verse (Psalms 139:5) ״אָחוֹר וָקֶדֶם צַרְתָּנִי״, which our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Genesis Rabbah ch. 1): אָחוֹר — last of all the deeds, וָקֶדֶם — first of the deed of Creation, as it is written: ״וְרוּחַ אֱלֹהִים מְרַחֶפֶת״ etc. — see then, that there are two formations in man: the formation of the spirit and the formation of the body. Now, at the time of the emission of seed, our Sages of blessed memory said (Zohar, Kedoshim 71a) that according to the intention that the one emitting seed holds, he draws down the soul for the seed: if he thinks evil and impure thoughts, he draws an impure soul to the drop; and if he thinks in purity, he draws a holy soul. Go and learn (Berakhot 10a; Yerushalmi Sanhedrin 10:2) from the sons of the wondrous righteous man, Hezekiah the king, upon him be peace, who married the daughter of a prophet, and because the woman thought about the servants of Merodach-bal-adan, she drew an evil soul to her two sons (Sanhedrin 104a). If so, the essential birth — which is the drawing down of the soul to the fetus — occurs at the time of the emission of seed, and it precedes it, for after the emission of seed, the power of thought that produces the seed has already preceded it. And this is the meaning of the verse <b>כִּי תַזְרִיעַ וְיָלְדָה</b>: Scripture informs us that the essential birth is at the time of the emission of seed — that when she emits seed, she has already given birth, and what will be will be: whether a pure soul or not, and there is no hope to reverse what already is. From this it follows that what a person accomplishes in the aspect of birth is at the time of the emission of seed, for then is the essential birth in terms of the soul — which is the primary element in man — and the occurrence of the matter and its coming into being, even though it is hidden, is called birth. And from the inner logic of these our words you will understand the saying (Genesis 12:5) ״וְאֶת הַנֶּפֶשׁ אֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ בְחָרָן״ — these are the souls they made through their coupling; even though Sarah did not give birth, with every single coupling they would bring forth souls, and even though no body was formed for them, the truth is not negated on that account. And His saying <b>זָכָר</b> alludes that there is also power in those who emit at the time of the emission of seed to produce a male, according to the intention they direct to draw a soul from the World of the Masculine (alma d'dichura)." "<b>Further,</b> Scripture alludes that since the woman is the principal [agent] in bearing children, Scripture was concerned on her behalf: since she is not obligated, she might not consent to childbearing on account of the suffering she endures; and she also is diminished in appearance through childbirth, and therefore might not wish to marry — and if she does marry, her intention would be only to fulfill her animal desire, for hers is the greater [desire] (Gittin 49a). Therefore Scripture comes and promises her that even though she is not commanded, if she acts in the order described she will have advantage and distinction like a male. And this is the meaning of His saying <b>אִשָּׁה כִּי תַזְרִיעַ</b> etc. — meaning, a woman who performs this act of producing seed, thereby removing the impediment to conjugal union; <b>וְיָלְדָה</b> — meaning, her intention at the time of her emission of seed is for the purpose of childbirth and not for animal desire. And had He said nothing but ״אִשָּׁה כִּי תֵלֵד״, the meaning would have been that He is speaking of whenever she gives birth — but that is not so; rather, it is one who directs her conjugal intention toward childbirth — this woman is on the level of a male. And this is His saying <b>זָכָר.</b> And He said <b>וְיָלְדָה</b> and not תֵלֵד, to say that she does not attain that status except after the matter comes into actual effect. Or perhaps it is to this that He intended in saying <b>לֵאמֹר</b> and adjoining to it the word <b>אִשָּׁה</b> — meaning, ״לֵאמֹר״ in the language of distinction and exaltation, for whom? — ״אִשָּׁה כִּי תַזְרִיעַ וְיָלְדָה״: and what is her distinction? — <b>זָכָר</b>, in all the aspects through which the male ranks above the woman. Or perhaps it is to this that He intended in the verse (Proverbs 10:1) ״וּבֵן כְּסִיל תּוּגַת אִמּוֹ״ — for the deficiency on account of what was mentioned affects her." "Further, the verse alludes to Kenesset Yisrael, whom we find is called a woman in the words of the Prophets, as it is written (Isaiah 54:5): ״כִּי בוֹעֲלַיִךְ עוֹשַׂיִךְ״, and it is written (Isaiah 54:6): ״וְאֵשֶׁת נְעוּרִים״, and it is written (Hosea 2:21): ״וְאֵרַשְׂתִּיךָ לִי״ etc., (Isaiah 50:1): ״אֵיזֶה סֵפֶר כְּרִיתוּת״ etc. And concerning her the verse says: ״אִשָּׁה כִּי תַזְרִיעַ״ — meaning the sowing of mitzvot and good deeds, after the manner of His saying (Hosea 10:12): ״זִרְעוּ לָכֶם לִצְדָקָה״. <b>וְיָלְדָה זָכָר</b> — meaning her birth-giving will be male, that is, know that the aspect of the male is a supernal aspect beyond that of the female, and the Torah assigns the supernal aspects to the aspect of the male. And the verse informs us that if Kenesset Yisrael sows, she will undoubtedly give birth to supersonic levels — and this is the statement of our Sages, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 98a, Shemot Rabbah 15), concerning the magnitude of the wondrous wonders that HaShem will perform at the coming of the Redeemer, if Israel merits through their worthy deeds. And the intention is to distinguish between the awaited redemption and the former redemption from Egypt, when Israel were bare and naked; moreover the verse says (Deuteronomy 4:34): ״גּוֹי מִקֶּרֶב גּוֹי״, and that redemption did not stand firm, for the Temple was destroyed and they were exiled, and what happened happened, and this good is not in the aspect of male. But the future redemption, inasmuch as it will at all events come about through the merit of Israel — even if they should not be worthy, nevertheless through the prolongation of exile and the engagement with Torah, as it is written (Deuteronomy 31:21): ״לֹא תִשָּׁכַח מִפִּי זַרְעוֹ״ — in any case the redemption will be in the aspect of male, and it will endure forever. And His saying <b>וְטָמְאָה</b> etc. — here is alluded a rectification that HaShem will effect to attain the aspect of the male, for during seven years the birth pangs of Mashiach will be set in motion, as they said (Sanhedrin 97a), of blessed memory: the Son of David comes during a seven-year period, etc., to chastise them and to purify them — and this is His saying ״וְטָמְאָה״: He ascribes the chastisement to the aspect of impurity, for He who is the impure one — He is the one who chastises, and He says שִׁבְעַת יָמִים — meaning seven years, after the manner of their saying (Ketubot 57a): ״תֵּשֵׁב הַנַּעֲרָה אִתָּנוּ יָמִים אוֹ עָשׂוֹר״, and they said, of blessed memory: ״יָמִים״ means a year. <b>וּבַיּוֹם הַשְּׁמִינִי</b> etc. — meaning that after the passage of seven years, at the beginning of the eighth day, the flesh of his foreskin will be circumcised, for then HaShem will remove the aspect of orlah from the world, as it is written: ״וְאֶת רוּחַ הַטֻּמְאָה אַעֲבִיר מִן הָאָרֶץ״, and this will be in the eighth year. And the aspect of orlah, that it is the kelipah, is well known."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:27.287468497Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}